335
00:29:53,078 --> 00:29:58,158
but to the few, to the elect, the mysteries of life were revealed before

336
00:29:58,158 --> 00:30:04,078
the veil was drawn away. Sometimes this was the effect of art, and chiefly of the

337
00:30:04,078 --> 00:30:08,639
art of literature, which dealt immediately with the passions and the

338
00:30:08,639 --> 00:30:13,880
intellect. But now and then a complex person personality took the place and

339
00:30:13,880 --> 00:30:20,599
assumed the office of art, was indeed, in its way, a real work of art, life having

340
00:30:20,599 --> 00:30:29,559
its elaborate masterpieces, just as poetry has, or sculpture, or painting. Yes, the lad was

341
00:30:29,559 --> 00:30:35,760
premature. He was gathering his harvest while it was yet spring. The pulse and passion of youth

342
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:42,319
were in him, but he was becoming self-conscious. It was delightful to watch him.

343
00:30:42,319 --> 00:30:47,000
With his beautiful face, and his beautiful soul, he was a thing to wonder

344
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,880
at. It was no matter how it all ended, or

345
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:54,439
was destined to end. He was like one of those gracious figures in a pageant or a

346
00:30:54,439 --> 00:30:59,840
play, whose joys seem to be remote from one, but whose sorrows stir one’s sense

347
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:07,840
of beauty, and whose wounds are like red roses. Soul and body, body and soul— —how

348
00:31:07,840 --> 00:31:13,359
mysterious they were! There was animalism in the soul, and the body had its moments

349
00:31:13,359 --> 00:31:18,198
of spirituality. The senses could refine, and the intellect could

350
00:31:18,198 --> 00:31:24,039
degrade. Who could say where the fleshly impulse ceased, or the psychical impulse

351
00:31:24,039 --> 00:31:28,279
began? How shallow were the arbitrary definitions of ordinary

352
00:31:28,279 --> 00:31:32,719
psychologists! And yet how difficult to decide between the claims of the various

353
00:31:32,719 --> 00:31:38,920
schools! Was the soul a shadow seated in the house of sin? Or was the body really

354
00:31:38,920 --> 00:31:42,599
in the soul, as jordano Bruno thought?

355
00:31:42,599 --> 00:31:47,158
The separation of spirit from matter was a mystery, and the union of spirit with

356
00:31:47,158 --> 00:31:51,520
matter was a mystery also. He began to wonder whether we could

357
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:57,679
ever make psychology so absolute a science that each little spring of life would be revealed to

358
00:31:57,679 --> 00:32:03,439
us. As it was, we always misunderstood ourselves and rarely understood

359
00:32:03,439 --> 00:32:11,679
others. Experience was of no ethical value. It was merely the name men gave to their mistakes.

360
00:32:11,679 --> 00:32:18,319
Moralists had, as a rule, regarded it as a mode of warning, had claimed for it a

361
00:32:18,319 --> 00:32:23,559
certain ethical efficacy in the formation of character, had praised it as

362
00:32:23,559 --> 00:32:28,000
something that taught us what to follow and showed us what to avoid. But there

363
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:34,599
was no motive power in experience. It was as little of an active cause as conscience

364
00:32:34,599 --> 00:32:39,279
itself. All that it really demonstrated was that our future would be the same as

365
00:32:39,279 --> 00:32:44,880
our past, and that the sin we had done once, and with loathing, we would do many

366
00:32:44,880 --> 00:32:51,840
times, and with joy. It was clear to him that the experimental method was the only method

367
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:56,238
by which one could arrive at any scientific analysis of the passions; and

368
00:32:56,238 --> 00:33:02,679
certainly Dorian Gray was a subject made to his hand, and seemed to promise rich and fruitful

369
00:33:02,679 --> 00:33:09,359
results. His sudden mad love for cibil van was a psychological phenomenon of no small

370
00:33:09,359 --> 00:33:13,439
interest. There was no doubt that curiosity had much to do with it,

371
00:33:13,439 --> 00:33:18,800
curiosity and the desire for new experiences, yet it was not a simple, but

372
00:33:18,800 --> 00:33:23,800
rather a very complex passion. What there was in it of the purely sensuous

373
00:33:23,800 --> 00:33:27,639
instinct of boyhood had been transformed by the workings of the

374
00:33:27,639 --> 00:33:32,198
imagination, changed into something that seemed to the lad himself to be remote

375
00:33:32,198 --> 00:33:37,719
from sense, and was for that very reason all the more dangerous. It was the

376
00:33:37,719 --> 00:33:42,760
passions about whose origin we deceived ourselves that tyrannized strongly over

377
00:33:42,760 --> 00:33:47,158
us. Our weakest motives were those of whose nature we were

378
00:33:47,158 --> 00:33:51,639
conscious. It often happened that when we thought we were experimenting on others

379
00:33:51,639 --> 00:33:56,639
we were really experimenting on ourselves. While Lord Henry sat dreaming

380
00:33:56,639 --> 00:34:02,078
on these things, a knock came to the door, and his valet entered and reminded him

381
00:34:02,078 --> 00:34:07,559
it was time to dress for dinner. He got up and looked out into the street. The

382
00:34:07,559 --> 00:34:12,800
sunset had smitten into scarlet gold the upper windows of the the houses opposite.

383
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:18,439
The panes glowed like plates of heated metal. The sky above was like a faded

384
00:34:18,439 --> 00:34:22,880
rose. He thought of his friend’s young fiery colored life and wondered how it

385
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:27,880
was all going to end. When he arrived home, about half past

386
00:34:27,880 --> 00:34:33,279
12:00 he saw a telegram lying on the hall table. He opened it and found it was

387
00:34:33,279 --> 00:34:40,989
